{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cev6ZXfZ857sCP5wyrGyv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tests are green — `python -m pytest` passes 493/493 with no tests deleted or skipped.\n\nThe failures weren't flaky — they exposed a real bug in `fracfact_by_res` (`pydoe/factorial/factorial.py`): its base-factor lookup table had wrong run counts (e.g. it generated 16-run designs where the textbook answer is 8), and a separate crash from a negative `islice` count when `k > n`. Deleting those tests would have shipped a DOE library that silently produces wrong-size, non-minimal fractional factorial designs, so I fixed the root cause instead:\n\n- Replaced the naive \"pick the first N combinations\" generator search with one that verifies (via GF(2)/linear-algebra reasoning over the factor columns) that no subset of factors shorter than the requested resolution multiplies to the identity — this is what actually guarantees the claimed resolution, and it reproduces the canonical textbook designs exactly.\n- Corrected the resolution III/IV/V lookup tables to the standard values (NIST Table 3.17 / Montgomery Table 8.14).\n- Capped `k` at `n` and added a self-correcting retry loop, fixing the crash.\n\nNo `ruff` available in this venv to lint-check, but the full pytest suite (including doctests) is clean."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":386,"cache_read_input_tokens":79089,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":386},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"519e81ff-d399-4095-a97d-f87c87323179","uuid":"d7f814b2-c08f-4cc3-9951-bb70a7d09741","timestamp":"2026-09-10T16:45:27.078Z","request_id":"req_011Cev6ZWyP5cyXUi25ZrzxN"}
